    <!--
        The <database> entries are load-bearing, not decoration.

        Nextcloud's OC\DB\MigrationService sets `checkOracle = true` for any app
        whose <dependencies> block declares NO <database> at all. With that flag
        on, every migration is validated against ensureOracleConstraints(), and
        that rule rejects a default-named primary key on any table whose
        unprefixed name is 23 characters or longer. Ten Keepiq tables are, among
        them keepiq_certificate_metadata (27), keepiq_passkey_credentials (26)
        and keepiq_team_folder_members (26).

        Without these entries `occ app:enable keepiq` ABORTS on a fresh install:
        the app is not installable from scratch on a stock Nextcloud at all. It
        survives on long-lived dev instances only because the checks apply
        exclusively to tables that do not exist yet.

        Declaring the databases the app actually supports is Nextcloud's own
        mechanism for this, and it is simply true: Keepiq has never supported
        Oracle, and its docker-compose, CI and every deployment run PostgreSQL,
        MySQL/MariaDB or SQLite. It also matches the rest of the fleet, where
        openregister and opencatalogi ship exactly these three entries.

        The table prefix moved from `doriath_` to `keepiq_` on 2026-09-08,
        together with the 78 index names, in the single consolidated migration
        lib/Migration/Version001000Date20260908000000.php. That migration renames
        the tables in place before the schema comparison runs, so existing
        installs keep their rows; on PostgreSQL it also renames the sequences and
        primary-key constraints, which a table rename leaves behind.

        An earlier revision of this comment argued the prefix should stay frozen.
        That reasoning was sound while the tables were merely inconsistent with
        the app id. It was overtaken by the decision to ship the fleet with one
        name per app, taken ahead of the App Store launch.
    -->
    <dependencies>
        <php min-version="8.3"/>
        <database min-version="10">pgsql</database>
        <database>sqlite</database>
        <database min-version="8.0">mysql</database>
        <!--
            Floor is 32, fleet-wide, so PHP 8.3 is guaranteed by the platform.

            31 was never actually deliverable: the CI matrix is derived from
            this `<nextcloud>` range (no leg below 32), and OpenRegister, whose
            integrations this app offers when it is present, declares
            `min-version="32"` itself. Advertising 31 in the App Store promised
            a range no test leg covered.

            max-version is 35 as of 2026-09-15: verified against Nextcloud 35
            (35.0.0 dev) on the shared dev instance — keepiq enabled, its DB
            migrations ran cleanly under `occ upgrade`, and the log showed no
            deprecations or errors. The optional openregister and integriq also
            declare 35 on development (verified 2026-09-24); Keepiq does not
            need either (ADR-006).
        -->
        <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="35"/>
    </dependencies>

